## Structure

| Path | Role | May import |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `domain/entities.py` | Plain dataclasses and the rules over their own state | the standard library |
| `domain/ports.py` | The Protocol the application depends on | `domain/entities.py` |
| `domain/enums.py` | Values only this module speaks | the standard library |
| `application/use_cases.py` | Orchestration, typed against the port | `domain/`, `jfastframework.errors` |
| `infrastructure/orm.py` | The SQLAlchemy table and the row → entity mapping | SQLAlchemy, `domain/` |
| `infrastructure/repository.py` | The adapter implementing the port | SQLAlchemy, `domain/` |
| `adapters/http.py` | The FastAPI router and its wire schemas | FastAPI, `application/`, `infrastructure/` |
| `public.py` | The only file other modules may import. Takes a session and `tenant_id`, returns DTOs | `application/`, `infrastructure/`, `domain/` |

The arrow points inward and never turns around. `adapters` knows
`application`, `application` knows the port, and the domain knows nothing.
`infrastructure` also points inward: it imports the domain to implement the
port, and the domain does not know it exists.

`adapters/http.py` is the one file that names both a use case and a concrete
adapter, in `get_use_cases`. That is a composition root, and keeping it to two
lines is what keeps `application/` free of infrastructure.

## The port

```python
class {{ Module }}Repository(Protocol):
    async def list(self, *, limit: int, offset: int) -> {{ Module }}Page: ...
    async def get(self, item_id: int) -> {{ Module }} | None: ...
{% for unique in uniques %}
    async def {{ unique.port_finder }}(self, {{ unique.parameters }}) -> {{ Module }} | None: ...
{% endfor %}
    async def add(self, entity: {{ Module }}) -> {{ Module }}: ...
    async def save(self, entity: {{ Module }}) -> {{ Module }}: ...
    async def remove(self, item_id: int) -> None: ...
```

Two adapters implement it: `Sql{{ Module }}Repository` against PostgreSQL, and
`InMemory{{ Module }}Repository` in `tests/__init__.py`. If the second one is
hard to write, the port is asking for something only a database can do — which
means the application layer has learned about databases again.

When a use case needs a query the port does not offer, add a method to the
port and implement it in both adapters. Do not reach past it.

## Why this layout and not layered

The domain and its rules run with no database, no event loop and no web
framework. `tests/test_{{ module }}_domain.py` proves it in a subprocess
rather than claiming it, and the check fails if anyone adds an eager import of
the router to `modules/{{ module }}/__init__.py`.

The cost is four directories and a mapping layer for what is otherwise CRUD.
Worth it when the rules are the hard part; not worth it when the module is a
table with a router on top. Use `--layout layered` for those.

